WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:07.200
Speaker 1: Let's go to Spencer from&nbsp;
California. Spencer, you're on next.&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:14.400
Speaker 5: Hey, David. First-Time Caller. Pleasure&nbsp;
to be on. And one of the things I'm wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:21.120
is I was watching one of the interviewers,&nbsp;
the interviews that you did last week where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:28.880
the interviewee, I think, raised the possibility&nbsp;
of having a third political party forming in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.920
U.S. sometime in the next couple of years. So&nbsp;
I'm wondering what your thoughts are on that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:39.840
And if you think that that's a feasible thing.
Speaker 1: I am very supportive of third parties.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.720
I think we are so myopically limited in the US by&nbsp;
having only two parties. I've also said that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.080
in order for third parties to really be viable&nbsp;
in the United States, we need to move in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:01:00.560
direction of ideally some kind of ranked choice or&nbsp;
transferable voting system where people wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:07.360
worry. If I vote third party, I might be helping&nbsp;
the person I'm the most against. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.400
if as someone on the left you vote for the Green&nbsp;
Party, you might actually help the Republican by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.960
taking votes from the Democrats. So we need some&nbsp;
kind of transferable or ranked choice system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.760
number one and number two, we need to go towards&nbsp;
public financing of elections that will even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:27.040
the playing field in terms of what is able to be&nbsp;
spent by parties because initially third parties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.680
would be at a massive financial disadvantage.&nbsp;
So I support third parties. I would love to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.160
have strong parties in the United States other&nbsp;
than Democrats and Republicans. Without the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:43.920
reforms I mentioned, I don't know how it happens.
Speaker 5: OK. Yeah, that makes sense. And then I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.840
guess just another quick question, though, I've&nbsp;
been following your channel for a while and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:56.880
see all these clips of people like Mike Pilou and&nbsp;
Rudy Giuliani. Giuliani always talking about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.520
evidence is supposedly coming out soon,&nbsp;
very senior fraud cases or whatever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.080
Yeah. So I'm wondering, do you think they're&nbsp;
eventually going to pivot away from that as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.200
they approach the next election cycle&nbsp;
and just focus on winning elections?&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.200
Speaker 1: I don't know. It almost I almost am&nbsp;
wondering at this point if they'll just keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.280
saying the evidence is just around the corner&nbsp;
until they're dead. I don't know. They don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.840
seem to have any off ramp from repeating that&nbsp;
we're about to get all the evidence they.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.880
It's bizarre. I don't know. I don't&nbsp;
know when they're going to stop talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.040
about it.
Speaker 5:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.040
Got you. Thanks for taking my call, David.
Speaker 1: All right, thanks, Spencer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:39.760
Great to hear from you. That's Spencer from&nbsp;
California. Very powerful series of callers today.

